  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3}:$ \nSince $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4}.$ This means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{15}:$\nSince $9<15<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{15}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n1<\\sqrt{3}<2<\\sqrt{7}<3<\\sqrt{15}<4\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$",
